/**
 * Gold-Standard Few-Shot Examples for AI Coach
 * 20 curated ideal coaching responses across different position types and skill levels.
 * These are injected into the LLM prompt to improve response consistency and quality.
 */

export interface GoldStandardExample {
  id: string;
  positionType: "tactical" | "strategic" | "endgame" | "opening" | "blunder";
  skillLevel: "beginner" | "intermediate" | "advanced";
  scenario: string;
  idealResponse: string;
}

export const GOLD_STANDARD_EXAMPLES: GoldStandardExample[] = [
  // === TACTICAL EXAMPLES ===
  {
    id: "tac-fork-beginner",
    positionType: "tactical",
    skillLevel: "beginner",
    scenario: "User (800) played Bd3 but missed a knight fork on e6 that wins the queen.",
    idealResponse: `Your move Bd3 develops the bishop, which isn't bad on its own. But there was a much stronger move hiding here!

**Threats:** Your opponent's queen on d8 and rook on a8 are both unprotected right now.

**Best Move:** Ne6 was the winning move. Here's why: your knight jumps to e6, and suddenly it attacks BOTH the queen on d8 and the rook on a8 at the same time. Your opponent can only save one of them, so you win the rook for free!

**Concept:** This pattern is called a "fork" — it's when one piece attacks two (or more) enemy pieces at the same time. Knights are especially good at forks because they jump in an L-shape, which makes them hard to see coming. Look for knight forks whenever your opponent has two unprotected pieces!`,
  },
  {
    id: "tac-pin-intermediate",
    positionType: "tactical",
    skillLevel: "intermediate",
    scenario: "User (1300) played h3 but missed Bb5 pinning the knight to the king.",
    idealResponse: `h3 prevents Bg4 but misses a much stronger idea.

**Threats:** Black's knight on c6 is the key defender of e5. If we can neutralize it, our center collapses their position.

**Best Move:** Bb5 was stronger here — it pins the knight on c6 to the king. After Bb5, the knight can't move without exposing the king to check. This means Black loses control of e5, and after a future Bxc6 dxc6, their pawn structure is permanently damaged.

**Concept:** This is a pin — when a piece can't move because it would expose a more valuable piece behind it. Bishops are ideal for creating pins along diagonals. The combination of the pin + structural damage after the exchange is what makes this so effective.`,
  },
  {
    id: "tac-discovered-advanced",
    positionType: "tactical",
    skillLevel: "advanced",
    scenario: "User (1800) played Re1 but missed Nxf7 with a discovered attack on the queen.",
    idealResponse: `Re1 is reasonable prophylaxis, but Nxf7 wins material by force.

**Threats:** The knight sacrifice on f7 unleashes a discovered attack — moving the knight reveals your bishop's attack on Black's queen on d8.

**Best Moves:**
1. **Nxf7** (best) — Kxf7 is forced, then Bxd8 wins the queen. White emerges a full exchange up after Rxd8. The Stockfish line continues with Bc7 Rd7 Bb6 with a dominating position.
2. **Re1** (played) — Solid but passive. Eval drops from +3.2 to +0.8.

**Concept:** Discovered attack — the knight moves with tempo (threatening the king via the f7 capture) while simultaneously unveiling the bishop's line to d8. Always check for discovered attacks when you have pieces aligned on the same file, rank, or diagonal.`,
  },
  {
    id: "tac-backrank-beginner",
    positionType: "tactical",
    skillLevel: "beginner",
    scenario: "User (700) missed a back rank checkmate with Rd8#.",
    idealResponse: `You had a checkmate in one move here!

**Threats:** Look at your opponent's king on g8 — it's trapped on the back row. The pawns on f7, g7, and h7 are blocking its escape. That means if you can get a rook or queen to the 8th rank, it's game over!

**Best Move:** Rd8 was checkmate! Your rook slides to d8, and the king has nowhere to go — the pawns block all the escape squares. That's an instant win!

**Concept:** This is called a "back rank checkmate" — one of the most important patterns in chess. It happens when the king is stuck behind its own pawns with no escape. Always check: can my rook or queen reach the back rank? And watch out for it happening to YOUR king too — that's why it's often smart to play h3 or g3 to give your king a "window" to escape.`,
  },

  // === STRATEGIC EXAMPLES ===
  {
    id: "strat-pawnstruct-intermediate",
    positionType: "strategic",
    skillLevel: "intermediate",
    scenario: "User (1400) captured cxd5 creating isolated queen's pawn vs Nxd5 keeping structure intact.",
    idealResponse: `cxd5 isn't terrible, but Nxd5 was strategically cleaner.

**Plans:** After cxd5, you now have an isolated d-pawn. That means no other pawn can support it, so your opponent can target it by putting a knight on d5 or a rook on the d-file. You'll spend moves defending it rather than attacking.

**Best Move:** Nxd5 keeps your pawn structure intact. Your knight recaptures on a strong central square, and you maintain your pawn chain. From d5, the knight controls key squares (c3, e3, f4, f6, b4, b6), and if Black takes it, your pawn retakes toward the center.

**Piece Roles:** After Nxd5, your knight becomes your best piece — centralized, well-supported, and hard to dislodge. After cxd5, that same d-pawn becomes a liability you have to babysit.

**Concept:** Isolated pawns (called "isolani") are pawns with no friendly pawns on adjacent files. They can be targets in the endgame. The general rule: avoid creating them unless you get very active piece play in return.`,
  },
  {
    id: "strat-pieceactivity-advanced",
    positionType: "strategic",
    skillLevel: "advanced",
    scenario: "User (1900) played a4 but the engine preferred Nd2-f1-e3 maneuver.",
    idealResponse: `a4 has some merit in space-gaining, but Nd2 initiating the f1-e3 maneuver is the key plan here.

**Plans:** The knight on b1 (or d2) needs to reach e3, where it controls d5, f5, c4, and g4 — all critical squares. The path is Nd2-Nf1-Ne3. This is a standard regrouping in this structure that transforms your worst piece into your best.

**Piece Roles:**
- Knight on d2: currently passive with limited scope
- After Ne3: controls the d5 outpost, supports a future f4 break, and eyes both kingside and center
- Your dark-squared bishop is already active; the knight is the piece that needs improvement

**Concept:** "Improve your worst piece" — a fundamental strategic principle. Before launching any pawn advance, ask: which piece is my worst, and can I improve it? The Nd2-f1-e3 maneuver is one of the most thematic regrouping sequences in these structures (seen in the Spanish, Italian, and many d4 openings).`,
  },
  {
    id: "strat-space-intermediate",
    positionType: "strategic",
    skillLevel: "intermediate",
    scenario: "User (1200) played a3 in a position where d4 opens the center against an uncastled king.",
    idealResponse: `a3 is a waiting move that doesn't do much. The position called for action!

**Threats:** Your opponent hasn't castled yet — their king is still in the center on e8. This is a big deal. When the opponent's king is stuck in the center, you want to open lines as fast as possible.

**Best Move:** d4 was the right move. It opens the center immediately. After d4 exd4 Nxd4, your knight lands on a powerful square, you open the d-file for your rooks, and your opponent's king is suddenly exposed to all your pieces.

**Plans:** The plan after d4 is simple: open the center → activate your pieces → attack the uncastled king. Every tempo matters when the king is exposed.

**Concept:** When your opponent hasn't castled, opening the center is almost always correct. Think of it like this: their king is standing in the middle of a battlefield, and you want to tear down all the walls (pawns) protecting it.`,
  },

  // === ENDGAME EXAMPLES ===
  {
    id: "end-kingactivity-beginner",
    positionType: "endgame",
    skillLevel: "beginner",
    scenario: "User (900) kept their king on g1 in a king+pawn endgame instead of marching it to the center.",
    idealResponse: `In the endgame, your king needs to come out and fight!

**Best Move:** Kf2 was the right idea — start walking your king toward the center. In a king and pawn endgame, the king is like a strong piece. It can attack pawns, support your own pawns, and control important squares.

**Plans:** The plan is: Kf2 → Ke3 → Kd4. Once your king reaches d4, it controls the center and supports your d-pawn's advance. Your opponent's king on g8 is far away and can't stop both your king and your pawn.

**Concept:** "Activate your king in the endgame" — this is one of the most important rules in chess. During the middlegame, the king hides in the corner for safety. But once most pieces are traded off, the king becomes a powerful fighter. Bring it to the center as fast as possible!`,
  },
  {
    id: "end-passedpawn-intermediate",
    positionType: "endgame",
    skillLevel: "intermediate",
    scenario: "User (1500) played Rc2 but missed creating a passed pawn with b5.",
    idealResponse: `Rc2 is passive — your rook belongs behind passed pawns, but first you need to create one!

**Best Move:** b5 was the key move. After b5 axb5 axb5, you create a protected passed pawn on b5. Black's rook will be tied down to stopping it, freeing your king and rook for active play.

**Plans:** After b5 axb5 axb5: 1) Advance the b-pawn to b6, 2) Put your rook behind it on b1 or a1, 3) March your king to support the pawn. Black has to choose between stopping the pawn and defending the kingside.

**Piece Roles:** Your rook should go BEHIND the passed pawn (on the b-file), not sideways. Rooks are strongest behind passed pawns because they gain scope as the pawn advances.

**Concept:** "Passed pawns must be pushed" (Aron Nimzowitsch). A passed pawn — one with no enemy pawns blocking or guarding its path — is a powerful weapon in the endgame. Create them and push them!`,
  },
  {
    id: "end-opposition-advanced",
    positionType: "endgame",
    skillLevel: "advanced",
    scenario: "User (1700) played Ke5 losing the opposition in a K+P vs K endgame.",
    idealResponse: `Ke5 loses the opposition and lets Black draw. Ke4 was the only winning move.

**Best Moves:**
1. **Ke4** (wins) — Maintains the opposition. After ...Ke6, Kd4 followed by Kc5 penetrates decisively.
2. **Ke5** (played, draws) — After ...Ke7, Black maintains the opposition and holds the draw by mirroring your king's movements.

**Concept:** The opposition is when two kings face each other with one square between them, and it's the other player's turn to move. The player who does NOT have to move holds the opposition — they can mirror and block the opponent's king. In K+P endgames, this often determines the entire game. Key rule: approach with your king in front of the pawn, not beside it, to secure the opposition.`,
  },

  // === OPENING EXAMPLES ===
  {
    id: "open-develop-beginner",
    positionType: "opening",
    skillLevel: "beginner",
    scenario: "User (600) played Qh5 on move 3, bringing the queen out early.",
    idealResponse: `I see you brought your queen out to h5 early! While it looks aggressive, this can actually cause problems.

**Threats:** Your queen on h5 looks scary, but your opponent can develop pieces while attacking it. For example, Nc6 defends and develops, then Nf6 attacks your queen directly.

**Best Move:** In the opening, it's better to develop your knights and bishops first. Nf3 or Bc4 would develop a piece toward the center while keeping your queen safe at home for now.

**Plans:** The opening plan is: 1) Move center pawns (e4, d4), 2) Develop knights and bishops, 3) Castle to get your king safe, 4) THEN bring your queen out. Your queen is too valuable to risk early — every time your opponent attacks it, you lose a turn retreating while they develop.

**Concept:** "Don't bring your queen out too early" — experienced players can gain time by developing their pieces while chasing your queen around the board. Develop the smaller pieces first, castle, then the queen joins the battle.`,
  },
  {
    id: "open-center-intermediate",
    positionType: "opening",
    skillLevel: "intermediate",
    scenario: "User (1300) played the Italian Game and is asking about the plans after 4.d3.",
    idealResponse: `The Italian Game with 4.d3 — the Giuoco Piano ("quiet game"). Solid choice!

**Plans:** The main plan in the d3 Italian is:
1. Castle kingside for king safety
2. Play c3 preparing d4, which would establish an ideal pawn center
3. Develop the knight from b1 to d2, then reroute it to f1-g3 or f1-e3
4. Build toward a kingside attack with pieces or a central break

**Piece Roles:** Your bishop on c4 eyes f7 (the weakest square in Black's camp). Your knight should aim for g3 or e3 via f1. The bishop on c1 often goes to e3 or g5, depending on Black's setup.

**Concept:** The Giuoco Piano is about slow, methodical preparation. Unlike the sharp Evans Gambit or Fried Liver, you're building a strong center first and looking for the right moment to strike. Masters like Caruana and Carlsen play this regularly at the highest level.`,
